### 7. Best Practices

**Wrap Unsafe Boundaries**
Never sprinkle `extern` calls throughout business logic. Write a thin, idiomatic Zig module that imports the unsafe symbols and exposes safe functions with Zig types, error handling, and slices. For example, wrap `libc`’s `malloc` and `free` into a Zig allocator (the standard library already does this with `std.heap.c_allocator`). This isolates ABI concerns and makes the rest of your code pure Zig.

**Use `@as` and Explicit Coercion at the Boundary**
When calling a C function, explicitly convert arguments with `@as(c_int, x)` or `@intCast`. This documents the ABI decision and catches overflow at the boundary.

**Manage Memory Ownership Explicitly**
If a C function returns a pointer that must be freed, document who owns it and use `defer` to call the appropriate deallocation function. If you pass Zig-allocated memory to C, ensure the allocator is compatible (e.g., a C function that calls `free()` on a pointer you gave it must have been obtained from `malloc`, not from a Zig `GeneralPurposeAllocator`).

**For Inline Assembly: Always Provide a Fallback**
Unless you are writing for a single, known target, provide a pure-Zig implementation of the same function and select the assembly version at comptime using `builtin.cpu.arch`. This keeps your code portable.

```zig
fn popcount(word: u32) u32 {
    if (builtin.cpu.arch == .x86_64 and std.Target.x86.featureSetHas(builtin.cpu.features, .popcnt)) {
        var out: u32 = undefined;
        asm volatile ("popcntl %[in], %[out]"
            : [out] "=r" (out),
            : [in] "r" (word),
        );
        return out;
    }
    // Fallback for other architectures.
    return @popCount(word);
}
```

**Use `volatile` Sparingly**
Only use `volatile` when the assembly has side effects that must not be elided (I/O ports, special instructions like `cpuid`, fences). For purely computational asm, omit `volatile` so the optimizer can delete dead code and reorder.

**Name Your Inputs and Outputs**
Zig’s named operand syntax `[eax] "{eax}" (val)` makes constraints self-documenting. Avoid positional `%0`, `%1` which are brittle and unreadable.

